ARTICLES = {
    'dealership-lead-response-guide': {
        'title': 'The Dealership Lead Response Guide: From Enquiry to Next Action',
        'meta': 'A practical guide to dealership lead routing, response time, follow-up ownership and reducing enquiry leakage.',
        'description': 'A practical operating framework for making sure every dealership enquiry receives a clear owner and next action.',
        'read': '9 min read',
        'sections': [
            ('Why dealership lead response breaks down', '<p>Lead response is rarely a single-person problem. It is a hand-off problem. Website forms, phone calls, finance enquiries, marketplace leads and WhatsApp messages often enter through different routes. Without a central workflow, the dealership depends on whoever notices first.</p><p>The result is not always an obviously “lost lead”. More often it is a slow first response, an incomplete customer record, no follow-up date or a conversation that cannot be seen by the sales manager.</p>'),
            ('The minimum information every lead needs', '<ul><li>A named owner or responsible team</li><li>The source and campaign that generated the enquiry</li><li>The vehicle, service or outcome the customer wants</li><li>The last contact and exact next action</li><li>A follow-up deadline visible to the team</li></ul>'),
            ('A practical response workflow', '<ol><li><strong>Capture:</strong> create one record with customer and source context.</li><li><strong>Route:</strong> assign by branch, vehicle, campaign or workload.</li><li><strong>Respond:</strong> record the first meaningful contact, not only an automated acknowledgement.</li><li><strong>Qualify:</strong> identify timing, finance, trade-in and vehicle fit.</li><li><strong>Advance:</strong> move the opportunity only when a real next step exists.</li><li><strong>Review:</strong> give managers visibility into ageing and overdue actions.</li></ol>'),
            ('What dealership owners should measure', '<p>Response time matters, but it is not enough. Owners should also track assignment time, follow-up completion, pipeline ageing, contactability, appointment progression, source quality and final outcomes. The goal is not a dashboard full of numbers. It is early warning when the sales process is leaking.</p>'),
            ('Where DealershipCore fits', '<p>DealershipCore is designed to connect lead capture, ownership, follow-up and reporting around one visible workflow. The platform does not replace sales skill. It makes the work and the next action harder to lose.</p>'),
        ],
    },
    'dealership-software-buyers-guide': {
        'title': 'Dealership Software Buyer’s Guide for South African Independent Dealers',
        'meta': 'How to compare dealership management software, CRM, stock tools, websites, valuation funnels and reporting for a South African dealership.',
        'description': 'The questions dealership owners should ask before choosing or replacing dealership software.',
        'read': '12 min read',
        'sections': [
            ('Start with the operating problem—not the software category', '<p>A dealership may think it needs a CRM when the real problem is lead routing. It may think it needs a new website when the real problem is stale stock data. It may think it needs more reports when the real problem is inconsistent data entry.</p><p>Write down the operational moments where information is lost, delayed or duplicated before comparing product lists.</p>'),
            ('The core software layers', '<table><thead><tr><th>Layer</th><th>What it should control</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>DMS / administration</td><td>Stock, transactions, compliance and operational records</td></tr><tr><td>CRM / lead management</td><td>Enquiries, communication, pipeline and follow-up</td></tr><tr><td>Website and funnels</td><td>Stock discovery, valuation, finance and lead capture</td></tr><tr><td>Reporting</td><td>Owner visibility, accountability and decisions</td></tr><tr><td>Automation</td><td>Routing, reminders, alerts and repetitive hand-offs</td></tr></tbody></table>'),
            ('Questions to ask every provider', '<ul><li>Can we see every new lead in one place?</li><li>Can the system prove who owns the next action?</li><li>Does stock remain aligned with the website?</li><li>Can owners compare branches, teams and sources?</li><li>What is included, what is an add-on and what is still roadmap?</li><li>How is data exported if we leave?</li><li>Which integrations are native and which require custom work?</li></ul>'),
            ('Do not confuse a long feature list with a connected workflow', '<p>Two systems can list the same features while creating very different daily experiences. Ask the provider to demonstrate one complete journey: a valuation lead enters, is assigned, receives follow-up, becomes an opportunity, connects to a vehicle and appears in owner reporting.</p>'),
            ('A fair comparison approach', '<p>Established platforms may be stronger in accounting, workshop or compliance depth. Newer platforms may be stronger in cloud workflows, website conversion, automation or owner visibility. The right choice depends on what the dealership needs now and what must remain integrated.</p>'),
        ],
    },
    'vehicle-valuation-lead-funnel': {
